1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,146
Hi, welcome to another
episode of the Earth Docs.

2
00:00:04,261 --> 00:00:06,130
Here is a fact that sounds backwards:

3
00:00:06,827 --> 00:00:10,844
the ice sheets of the Northern Hemisphere
were not destroyed by a catastrophe.

4
00:00:11,447 --> 00:00:13,735
They were destroyed by a
slightly warmer summer.

5
00:00:14,431 --> 00:00:17,484
According to a 2013 study
in the journal Nature,

6
00:00:17,809 --> 00:00:20,294
once the North American ice
sheet had grown large enough,

7
00:00:20,619 --> 00:00:24,613
a moderate increase in summer sunlight
pushed its mass balance negative,

8
00:00:24,938 --> 00:00:27,934
and it retreated almost completely
within a few thousand years.

9
00:00:28,746 --> 00:00:31,869
The bigger the ice got, the
readier it was to fail.

10
00:00:32,566 --> 00:00:35,352
That pattern has been running for
two and a half million years,

11
00:00:35,434 --> 00:00:37,222
according to UC Santa Barbara.

12
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:39,056
The growth was slow.

13
00:00:39,439 --> 00:00:40,728
The endings were fast.

14
00:00:41,424 --> 00:00:44,548
That lopsided rhythm, long
build and sudden failure,

15
00:00:44,722 --> 00:00:47,938
is written into sea level, into
the shape of the continents,

16
00:00:48,147 --> 00:00:49,888
into where you are allowed to live.

17
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:52,802
You are standing in one of
the warm intervals right now.

18
00:00:53,406 --> 00:00:57,075
The thing driving the whole pattern is
a clock nobody has finished reading.

19
00:00:57,516 --> 00:01:01,951
So let's take it apart, in order,
and see where the knowing stops.

20
00:01:02,764 --> 00:01:04,540
Earth does not spin upright.

21
00:01:05,051 --> 00:01:07,826
Its axis leans, and the lean changes.

22
00:01:08,523 --> 00:01:13,247
According to NASA Science, over the last
million years that tilt has swung between

23
00:01:13,294 --> 00:01:16,463
twenty-two point one degrees and
twenty-four point five degrees and back

24
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:20,004
again, on a cycle that takes
about forty-one thousand years.

25
00:01:20,701 --> 00:01:23,859
Today the axis sits at
twenty-three point four degrees,

26
00:01:24,068 --> 00:01:28,074
roughly halfway between the extremes,
and it is very slowly decreasing.

27
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:33,194
NASA notes that it was last at maximum
tilt about ten thousand years ago,

28
00:01:33,519 --> 00:01:36,340
and will reach its minimum about
ten thousand years from now.

29
00:01:37,454 --> 00:01:38,778
Why does a lean matter?

30
00:01:39,475 --> 00:01:43,724
Because it changes where the sunlight
lands, not how much the planet gets.

31
00:01:44,676 --> 00:01:49,332
A larger tilt means each hemisphere leans
further into the Sun in summer and further

32
00:01:49,390 --> 00:01:53,871
away in winter, so the seasons sharpen,
and the effect is strongest at high

33
00:01:53,941 --> 00:01:58,585
northern latitudes. Those are the
latitudes where ice sheets live or die.

34
00:01:59,537 --> 00:02:04,564
NASA states it plainly: larger tilt
angles favour periods of deglaciation.

35
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:09,823
Two point four degrees of total travel,
spread across forty-one thousand years.

36
00:02:10,636 --> 00:02:15,570
In a single human lifetime, the axis moves
so little you would need an instrument to

37
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:19,622
notice. And it helps decide
whether ice sits on top of Canada.

38
00:02:20,133 --> 00:02:22,745
But the tilt has been swinging
for millions of years.

39
00:02:23,349 --> 00:02:26,948
If it were the whole story, the ice
ages would beat like a metronome.

40
00:02:27,645 --> 00:02:31,441
Warm, cold, warm, cold, every
forty-one thousand years.

41
00:02:32,138 --> 00:02:35,261
They do not. So what does the wobble do?

42
00:02:35,957 --> 00:02:39,963
Stand outside on the third of January and
you are closer to the Sun than at any

43
00:02:40,009 --> 00:02:43,783
other moment of the year, about five
point one million kilometres closer,

44
00:02:43,933 --> 00:02:46,801
according to NASA. You are also freezing.

45
00:02:47,405 --> 00:02:52,060
That is precession, and it is the reason
the seasons and the orbit do not line up

46
00:02:52,107 --> 00:02:53,307
the way you would assume.

47
00:02:53,814 --> 00:02:56,542
It comes in two numbers,
and people mix them up.

48
00:02:57,146 --> 00:03:00,849
NASA Science puts the wobble of
the axis against the fixed stars,

49
00:03:01,174 --> 00:03:05,714
axial precession, at twenty-five thousand
seven hundred seventy-one point five

50
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:09,464
years. But the seasons cycle
against the orbit faster,

51
00:03:09,789 --> 00:03:13,701
in roughly twenty-one thousand years,
because the ellipse itself is rotating as

52
00:03:13,748 --> 00:03:19,181
the axis turns. A Milankovitch cycles
reference hosted by Harvard's school of

53
00:03:19,239 --> 00:03:21,202
engineering gives the same picture.

54
00:03:21,898 --> 00:03:25,068
About twenty-six thousand years
for the full turn of the axis.

55
00:03:25,579 --> 00:03:28,969
About twenty-one thousand years for the
beat between the seasons and the orbit.

56
00:03:29,665 --> 00:03:33,392
That distinction matters the moment you
try to match the cycle to the rock record.

57
00:03:34,205 --> 00:03:39,127
Watch what it does. NASA says
perihelion, our closest approach,

58
00:03:39,278 --> 00:03:42,459
currently falls around the
third of January, and aphelion,

59
00:03:42,552 --> 00:03:45,060
the farthest point, around
the fourth of July.

60
00:03:45,757 --> 00:03:50,064
That means each January, about six
point eight percent more incoming solar

61
00:03:50,110 --> 00:03:52,874
radiation reaches Earth
than it does each July.

62
00:03:53,384 --> 00:03:57,529
When the orbit is at its most elliptical,
that gap widens to about twenty-three

63
00:03:57,564 --> 00:04:01,256
percent. Precession does not
change the total sunlight.

64
00:04:01,581 --> 00:04:04,344
It changes the timing and the
intensity of the seasons,

65
00:04:04,623 --> 00:04:08,721
and it decides whether the northern summer
peak lands near the Sun or far from it.

66
00:04:09,417 --> 00:04:13,516
Now you have two real cycles, forty-one
thousand years and twenty-one thousand

67
00:04:13,574 --> 00:04:15,559
years, both measurable in the rock.

68
00:04:16,256 --> 00:04:18,334
Neither of them is one
hundred thousand years.

69
00:04:19,286 --> 00:04:22,061
So where does the one hundred
thousand year beat come from?

70
00:04:22,757 --> 00:04:24,034
The problem has a date.

71
00:04:24,731 --> 00:04:27,680
During a stretch called the
mid-Pleistocene transition,

72
00:04:28,063 --> 00:04:32,173
roughly one point two five million to
seven hundred thousand years ago according

73
00:04:32,208 --> 00:04:37,003
to a 2023 study in Nature Geoscience,
the rhythm of the ice ages changed.

74
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:41,554
Before it, the cycles were dominated
by forty-one thousand years.

75
00:04:42,251 --> 00:04:46,453
After it, strong one hundred thousand
year power appears in the climate record.

76
00:04:47,150 --> 00:04:51,086
And according to a study in Nature, that
switch happened without any apparent

77
00:04:51,132 --> 00:04:53,919
change in the insolation forcing.

78
00:04:54,268 --> 00:04:58,204
Of the three classic Milankovitch
parameters, only eccentricity has

79
00:04:58,285 --> 00:05:00,967
variations anywhere near
one hundred thousand years,

80
00:05:01,408 --> 00:05:05,344
and even there the reference gives a set
of terms between ninety-five thousand and

81
00:05:05,425 --> 00:05:09,512
one hundred thirty-six thousand years
that only loosely combine into the beat.

82
00:05:10,626 --> 00:05:15,375
The Milankovitch reference gives the range
as an eccentricity of zero point zero zero

83
00:05:15,444 --> 00:05:20,541
five to zero point zero five eight, with
a mean of zero point zero two eight.

84
00:05:21,145 --> 00:05:23,954
Today it sits at zero
point zero one seven.

85
00:05:24,558 --> 00:05:27,960
And there is the catch that has
annoyed researchers for fifty years.

86
00:05:28,656 --> 00:05:32,174
Eccentricity barely changes the
total energy Earth receives.

87
00:05:32,871 --> 00:05:37,700
NASA Science says the change in global
annual insolation from the eccentricity

88
00:05:37,758 --> 00:05:43,018
cycle is very small, and calls
eccentricity a relatively minor factor in

89
00:05:43,087 --> 00:05:48,010
seasonal climate. So the loudest rhythm in
the ice record appears to be paced by the

90
00:05:48,068 --> 00:05:50,413
quietest knob in the orbital system.

91
00:05:51,110 --> 00:05:53,560
How does a quiet knob write a loud beat?

92
00:05:54,372 --> 00:05:58,819
A piston corer comes up over the rail of
a research ship in the North Atlantic.

93
00:05:59,516 --> 00:06:04,845
Inside the tube is a column of grey mud,
layer on layer of microscopic shells that

94
00:06:04,915 --> 00:06:08,270
sank to the seafloor over
hundreds of thousands of years.

95
00:06:09,384 --> 00:06:13,053
Each layer records how much of the
planet's water was locked in ice at that

96
00:06:13,123 --> 00:06:18,881
moment. Here's how we know: in 2025, a
team led by Stephen Barker at Cardiff

97
00:06:18,951 --> 00:06:22,318
University published an analysis
in the journal Science.

98
00:06:23,130 --> 00:06:25,499
They took a million year
record of climate change,

99
00:06:25,824 --> 00:06:30,433
changes in the size of Northern Hemisphere
ice sheets and in deep ocean temperature,

100
00:06:30,758 --> 00:06:33,184
and matched it against the
shape of Earth's orbit,

101
00:06:33,428 --> 00:06:35,193
its wobble, and its tilt.

102
00:06:36,307 --> 00:06:39,036
Then they did something different
from everyone before them.

103
00:06:39,732 --> 00:06:42,647
Instead of trying to date
individual events precisely,

104
00:06:43,030 --> 00:06:45,897
they looked at the shape of the climate
record and asked how the orbital

105
00:06:45,955 --> 00:06:47,244
parameters fit together.

106
00:06:48,196 --> 00:06:50,820
According to UC Santa
Barbara's report on the work,

107
00:06:51,099 --> 00:06:54,477
the team found a clear imprint of the
different orbital parameters on the

108
00:06:54,512 --> 00:06:59,318
climate record. Every glaciation of
the past nine hundred thousand years,

109
00:06:59,493 --> 00:07:02,140
the study reports, follows
the same sequence.

110
00:07:02,836 --> 00:07:06,435
Deglaciation begins with the first
precession peak that starts while

111
00:07:06,493 --> 00:07:10,719
obliquity is rising, and that peak
arrives immediately after a minimum in

112
00:07:10,812 --> 00:07:15,550
eccentricity. Glacial inception then
follows with the next decrease in

113
00:07:15,596 --> 00:07:19,567
obliquity. One type of orbital
change ends the ice ages.

114
00:07:20,170 --> 00:07:21,370
Another brings them back.

115
00:07:22,063 --> 00:07:23,263
Notice what that does.

116
00:07:24,315 --> 00:07:27,287
Eccentricity never has to
force the climate directly.

117
00:07:27,891 --> 00:07:32,036
It only has to sit at its minimum,
damping the amplitude of precession,

118
00:07:32,210 --> 00:07:36,889
and let whichever precession peaks survive
become the ones that can end an ice age.

119
00:07:38,004 --> 00:07:41,916
On this reading, the one hundred thousand
year beat is a grouping rather than a

120
00:07:41,963 --> 00:07:46,885
pulse, a counting out of precession
cycles, gated by eccentricity.

121
00:07:47,698 --> 00:07:50,635
Barker's team called the
pattern largely deterministic,

122
00:07:50,961 --> 00:07:54,536
and used it to predict when each
interglacial of the past million years

123
00:07:54,583 --> 00:07:56,905
would occur, and how long it would last.

124
00:07:57,718 --> 00:07:59,586
But that is one strong result.

125
00:08:00,190 --> 00:08:01,792
It is not a settled field.

126
00:08:02,489 --> 00:08:06,413
The one hundred thousand year beat has a
habit of outrunning every explanation we

127
00:08:06,483 --> 00:08:12,137
hand it. A 2025 paper in Nature
Communications traces the same cycles to

128
00:08:12,253 --> 00:08:17,291
internal carbon, a coupling between ice
volume and the carbon cycle that recurs on

129
00:08:17,338 --> 00:08:19,985
a two point four million
year eccentricity rhythm,

130
00:08:20,426 --> 00:08:24,014
and that the authors argue amplifies
the one hundred thousand year signal.

131
00:08:24,710 --> 00:08:27,763
Others back a hypothesis
called obliquity skipping,

132
00:08:28,042 --> 00:08:33,232
described in that 2023 Nature Geoscience
paper, which holds that ice ages simply

133
00:08:33,290 --> 00:08:37,086
terminate every two or three of the
forty-one thousand year cycles.

134
00:08:37,783 --> 00:08:39,896
That averages out to
about a hundred thousand.

135
00:08:40,709 --> 00:08:42,705
Then there are the older candidates.

136
00:08:43,402 --> 00:08:48,255
In 1997, Richard Muller and Gordon
MacDonald proposed in the Proceedings of

137
00:08:48,289 --> 00:08:52,852
the National Academy of Sciences that the
one hundred thousand year cycle tracks the

138
00:08:52,945 --> 00:08:56,683
inclination of Earth's orbital
plane rather than eccentricity.

139
00:08:57,380 --> 00:09:01,084
Other researchers have since called
that an artifact of spectral analysis,

140
00:09:01,467 --> 00:09:04,485
so treat it as a live
disagreement, not an answer.

141
00:09:05,182 --> 00:09:10,185
Nicholas Shackleton's 2000 paper in
Science used oxygen isotopes trapped in

142
00:09:10,302 --> 00:09:14,005
Antarctic ice to separate ice
volume from deep water temperature.

143
00:09:14,702 --> 00:09:17,326
He found that at the one
hundred thousand year period,

144
00:09:17,569 --> 00:09:21,517
carbon dioxide and temperature sit
in phase with orbital eccentricity,

145
00:09:21,842 --> 00:09:24,222
while ice volume lags behind all three.

146
00:09:24,918 --> 00:09:29,516
That puts the carbon cycle, not the
ice, closer to the driver's seat.

147
00:09:29,868 --> 00:09:32,701
And then there is the record
that refuses to cooperate.

148
00:09:33,142 --> 00:09:37,182
The US Geological Survey studied
a calcite core from Devils Hole,

149
00:09:37,507 --> 00:09:39,841
a fault-created cave in the Nevada desert.

150
00:09:40,538 --> 00:09:44,601
The core is thirty-six centimetres
long, about the length of your forearm,

151
00:09:44,810 --> 00:09:47,272
and it holds five hundred
thousand years of climate,

152
00:09:47,597 --> 00:09:49,988
dated by uranium and thorium isotopes.

153
00:09:50,685 --> 00:09:54,807
According to the USGS, between three
hundred fifty thousand and four hundred

154
00:09:54,853 --> 00:09:59,462
fifty thousand years ago, when insolation
in both hemispheres barely changed at all,

155
00:09:59,788 --> 00:10:03,236
that record still shows a full
glacial and interglacial cycle.

156
00:10:03,932 --> 00:10:08,634
The USGS also notes that Devils Hole
suggests the last four interglacials each

157
00:10:08,692 --> 00:10:12,512
lasted around twenty thousand years,
twice the commonly assumed length.

158
00:10:13,209 --> 00:10:18,050
According to the USGS, this warm interval
has run about ten thousand years,

159
00:10:18,433 --> 00:10:22,670
though UC Santa Barbara dates the end
of the last glaciation to around eleven

160
00:10:22,717 --> 00:10:24,284
thousand seven hundred years ago.

161
00:10:24,888 --> 00:10:26,246
So the clock is still running.

162
00:10:26,571 --> 00:10:30,321
The Science team behind the
2025 analysis estimates that,

163
00:10:30,379 --> 00:10:34,327
on its own, Earth would shift toward
another ice age in roughly ten thousand

164
00:10:34,385 --> 00:10:37,589
years. Then they add
the part that matters.

165
00:10:38,285 --> 00:10:41,176
That transition is very
unlikely to arrive on schedule,

166
00:10:41,501 --> 00:10:45,542
because the carbon dioxide already in the
atmosphere has diverted the climate from

167
00:10:45,588 --> 00:10:49,919
its natural course. Which is the reason
the beat is worth knowing at all.

168
00:10:50,615 --> 00:10:55,143
Every coastline, every field, every city
you have ever stood in was built during

169
00:10:55,178 --> 00:10:58,231
the ten thousand years
since the ice last let go,

170
00:10:58,556 --> 00:11:02,608
in the gap between two beats, on a
clock we only partly understand.
